## 🛠️ Spring Boot 3.2 and HikariCP: Enabling Virtual Threads in Production

Spring Boot 3.2 makes enabling virtual threads for the entire web tier a one-property change:

```yaml
# application.yml — Spring Boot 3.2+
spring:
  threads:
    virtual:
      enabled: true
```

With this property set, Spring Boot configures:
- **Tomcat**: dispatches each HTTP request on a new virtual thread instead of the container's platform thread pool
- **Spring MVC `TaskExecutor`**: switches to `VirtualThreadTaskExecutor`
- **`@Async` methods**: execute on virtual threads automatically

Your controllers continue using standard synchronous Spring MVC — no reactive types, no `Mono`, no `Flux`. The virtual thread handles parking during DB and HTTP calls transparently.

```java
@RestController
public class UserController {
    @GetMapping("/users/{id}")
    public UserProfile getUser(@PathVariable String id) {
        User user = userRepository.findById(id);
        List<Order> orders = orderRepository.findByUser(id);
        return new UserProfile(user, orders);
    }
}
// With virtual threads enabled: both DB calls park the virtual thread, not the OS thread
```

**HikariCP compatibility note:** HikariCP 5.1+ (bundled in Spring Boot 3.2+) replaced all internal `synchronized` blocks with `ReentrantLock`. This means connection pool checkout and release — previously the most common cause of carrier thread pinning — are fully virtual-thread safe. If you are on an older HikariCP version (< 5.1), upgrade before enabling `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true`.

For Quarkus users, the equivalent is the `quarkus.virtual-threads.enabled=true` configuration. For Micronaut, virtual thread support is available via `micronaut.executors.io.type=virtual`.

